      "id": 1,
      "text": "Clinton Hill takes its name from Clinton Avenue, itself named after New York Governor DeWitt Clinton.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        4
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Clinton_Hill%2C_Brooklyn"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "Pratt Institute, founded in 1887 by Charles Pratt, sits in the heart of Clinton Hill and still shapes its artistic vibe.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        5,
        12
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia",
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Clinton_Hill%2C_Brooklyn"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "The Clinton Hill Historic District, designated in 1981, protects nearly 1,100 buildings, making it one of NYC’s largest contiguous landmarked areas.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        21
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://www.cityneighborhoods.nyc/clinton-hill"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "Historic brownstones and carriage houses line the streets, many restored by artists and writers who revived the neighborhood’s 19th‑century charm.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        20,
        23
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://www.cityneighborhoods.nyc/clinton-hill"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "The BMT and IND subway lines brought Irish, Italian, and later African‑American families to Clinton Hill, turning it into a diverse enclave.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        13
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://www.cityneighborhoods.nyc/clinton-hill"
    },
    {
